#### Server/Client Separation

See example: @src/lib/supabase/server.ts

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Server-only code
import 'server-only';
import { createSupabaseServerClient } from '@/lib/supabase/server';

export async function POST(request: Request) {
  const supabase = createSupabaseServerClient();
  // Server-side operations...
}

// ✅ Good: Client component (explicit)
'use client';

import { useState } from 'react';

export function InteractiveComponent() {
  // Client-side logic...
}
```

### Environment Variables & Secrets

#### Variable Naming
- `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` for safe client use (e.g., `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL`)
- Private keys for server-only code (e.g., `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY`)
- Never expose service role keys or private credentials on the client

#### Environment Variable Management
```typescript
// ✅ Good: Server-only variables
const apiKey = process.env.SERVER_ONLY_KEY; // Not NEXT_PUBLIC_*

// ❌ Bad: Public variables (exposed to client)
const apiKey = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_KEY; // Exposed to client
```

#### Hetzner Deployment
- Use Hetzner-specific environment variable configuration
- Document all required environment variables in `.env.example`
- Use Hetzner secrets management for production
- Ensure proper environment variable loading in deployment scripts

